[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Clean Architecture & Dependency Inversion in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Clean Architecture enforces the separation of concerns by dividing your application into distinct layers. The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) ensures that high-level modules do not depend on low-level modules, but both depend on abstractions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Layers of Clean Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Presentation Layer: Contains Activities, Fragments, and ViewModels. It handles UI rendering and user interactions."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Domain Layer: The core of the application. Contains business models and UseCases (Interactors). Completely independent of Android frameworks."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Layer: Implements repositories, local databases (Room), and remote APIs (Retrofit). It feeds data to the domain layer."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Define the Domain Model and Repository Interface"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the Domain layer, we define our plain Java object (POJO) and the interface for the repository. The Domain layer knows nothing about Retrofit or databases."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public class User {
    private String id;
    private String name;

    public User(String id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public String getId() { return id; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public interface UserRepository {
    User getUserById(String userId);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Implement the UseCase"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The UseCase encapsulates a single business action. It takes the repository interface via constructor injection, maintaining the Dependency Inversion Principle."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class GetUserUseCase {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    public GetUserUseCase(UserRepository userRepository) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
    }

    public User execute(String userId) {
        return userRepository.getUserById(userId);
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Implement the Data Layer"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Data layer implements the repository interface defined in the Domain layer. This is where dependency inversion happens: the data layer points inward toward the domain layer."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public class UserRepositoryImpl implements UserRepository {
    private final ApiService apiService;

    public UserRepositoryImpl(ApiService apiService) {
        this.apiService = apiService;
    }

    @Override
    public User getUserById(String userId) {
        // Fetch data from network or database and map to Domain User model
        UserEntity entity = apiService.fetchUser(userId);
        return new User(entity.getId(), entity.getFullName());
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Presentation Layer Integration"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Finally, the ViewModel or Presenter invokes the UseCase. By wiring dependencies carefully (or using a DI framework like Dagger/Hilt), your Android app becomes testable, maintainable, and decoupled."}]
